il the first successful load.\n\tcfg atomic.Pointer[config.Resolved]\n\n\t// initMu guards the lazily built shared parts below: one main committer,\n\t// one branch committer, one Closer, one executor, one Finisher and Reaper.\n\tinitMu sync.Mutex\n\tmc     *lazyCommitter\n\tbr     *lazyBranches\n\tcl     *closeout.Closer\n\texec   *local.Local\n\tfin    *reaper.Finisher\n\trp     *reaper.Reaper\n\tlim    *limits.Book\n\tbgt    *budget.Gate\n\t// budgetNow is the budget gate's clock; nil means time.Now (tests set it).\n\tbudgetNow func() time.Time\n\n\tmu      sync.Mutex\n\tclosers []func(context.Context)\n}\n\n// onClose registers fn to run when serve returns (committer worktrees, for\n// example). Closers run in reverse order.\n../docs/proposal.md:477:- **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's body also carries a per-job cost table (agent, tokens by type, cost, model, outcome and a link to the job's comment, PR) with a Total row, written by the `costs` loop. A reached daily cap is a `budget_capped` event. The repository may be public, so the spend it shows is public too; the table holds no agent text or host paths. Scanner failure streaks (#221, the early slice of the digest, #58): each scanner posts one comment on the status issue when a failure streak starts and one when it recovers. A streak starts at the first run that is a guard trip or guard error (reason: the guard rule name or `scan-error`), any other `scan_failed` run (a `timeout` counts as `scan_failed`) or a skip at `max_open`, and ends at the next `ok` run. The posted class is kept in the scanner's `meta` table (key `failure_streak`), so a restart does not repost, and it is written only after the post succeeds. Runs dropped at the global cap and `error` runs neither start nor end a streak. A comment carries the scanner name and the reason class only, never finding text, titles, fingerprints or log lines. The post goes through the effect layer, so a dry-run logs it (`scanner.streak_notice`) and stores no streak. While the status issue number is unknown nothing is posted and the streak is not recorded, so it posts once the issue exists.\n../docs/proposal.md:504:- **Publish.** A green `main` build starts `ard-build`, whose Jenkinsfile is not in this repository, so nothing merged here can reach its credentials. It builds the `Dockerfile` for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64` (no emulation: every `RUN` cross-compiles or downloads on the build platform), signs the digest with cosign, and pushes `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard:sha-<7>` (never re-pushed) and `:main`.\n../docs/proposal.md:505:- **Deploy.** A deploy job verifies the signature and the revision label, checks the new commit descends from the pinned one, and pins the digest and commit in the deployment repository. Argo CD renders this repository's `deploy/argocd` chart (the app of apps) with that pin and the site's values: one child Application per target repository, named `ard-<instance>`, reading `deploy/charts/ard-serve` at the pinned commit, so chart and image move together. The cluster admits no other Application from that chart.\n../docs/proposal.md:517:    P2 -->|\"Gate 3: 50% merged with no human code edits\"| P3[\"Phase 3: Scale<br/>k8s executor<br/>second repo<br/>shared roles repo<br/>budgets and digest\"]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0127ceLfQDHFjjp8Kr3e6nRa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
